  <section class="mb-9">
    <h2 class="text-[11px] uppercase tracking-wider text-[var(--color-text-dim)] font-medium mb-3">Research: epistemic modality and the maintenance of alternatives</h2>
    <p class="text-sm text-[var(--color-text-muted)] mb-4 max-w-prose">One linguistic question — does an epistemic possibility expression merely signal uncertainty, or does it also help maintain a content-specific alternative for subsequent reasoning? — made testable as a learnability question: whether the function can be acquired from developmentally-realistic linguistic input, and what evidence in that input it requires, calibrated against what the developmental literature knows about how it emerges in human cognition.</p>
    <div class="space-y-5">
      <div class="break-inside-avoid">
        <div class="flex justify-between gap-4 items-baseline">
          <p class="font-medium text-[15px]">Working paper on epistemic modality</p>
          <p class="text-sm text-[var(--color-text-dim)] whitespace-nowrap">in preparation</p>
        </div>
        <p class="text-sm mt-1">The linguistic account: speaker commitment, discourse update, and the maintenance of alternatives. States the competing analyses, what each predicts about the reasoning that follows an utterance, and the criteria — content-specificity, persistence, evidence-sensitivity — that separate a real maintenance effect from mention, priming, or generic uncertainty. The construct brief is currently under review by a formal semanticist, before any model or behavioural study.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="break-inside-avoid">
        <div class="flex justify-between gap-4 items-baseline">
          <p class="font-medium text-[15px]">MODUS — earlier language-model pilot</p>
          <p class="text-sm text-[var(--color-text-dim)] whitespace-nowrap">methods pilot</p>
        </div>
        <p class="text-sm mt-1">Controlled experiments on modal force, lexical form, evidence, and targeted intervention, developing methods to separate modal function from modal vocabulary: a direction built from evidence contrasts containing no modal words, tested by intervention against a base-rate control, with the lexical and generic-disruption confounds made explicit. Methodological groundwork for the alternative-maintenance experiments, not a result about modal semantics.</p>
        <p class="text-xs mt-1.5">
          <a href="https://xchuan-li.github.io/research/modus" class="text-[var(--color-accent)] hover:underline">Project page ↗</a>
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="break-inside-avoid">
        <div class="flex justify-between gap-4 items-baseline">
          <p class="font-medium text-[15px]">Developmental question</p>
          <p class="text-sm text-[var(--color-text-dim)] whitespace-nowrap">being scoped</p>
        </div>
        <p class="text-sm mt-1">How does this linguistic function become available in human cognition? Nonverbal modal reasoning and the acquisition of modal language have largely been studied separately; the question is what the modal expression itself does to subsequent reasoning when the available information is held constant. Being scoped against the existing developmental literature.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="mb-9">
    <h2 class="text-[11px] uppercase tracking-wider text-[var(--color-text-dim)] font-medium mb-3">Papers &amp; collaborations</h2>
    <p class="text-sm text-[var(--color-text-muted)] mb-4 max-w-prose">A first-author paper, and a collaboration — the concrete output behind the program, and where the engineering came from: controlled multilingual evaluation, intervention inside a model, and the analysis to read the result.</p>
    <div class="space-y-4 text-sm">
      <div class="break-inside-avoid">
        <p class="font-medium text-[15px]">Cross-Lingual Prototypicality Bias in Multimodal Evaluation Metrics and VLM Judges <span class="font-normal text-[var(--color-text-dim)]">· first-author paper</span></p>
        <p class="text-[var(--color-text-muted)] mt-0.5">A controlled cross-lingual audit of VLM judges and evaluation metrics: hold the image pair fixed, vary only the prompt language, and test whether the judge tracks meaning or a culture-specific prototype. ~12,600 judgments across seven languages and two VLM families; item-clustered robust regression, a 90-item human control, and a translation-cache integrity fault I found, re-ran in full, and reported. The same design logic the thesis applies to modal semantics: structure versus surface. Public code; revised version in preparation for arXiv.
          <a href="https://github.com/xchuan-li/cross_lingual_protobias" class="text-[var(--color-accent)] hover:underline whitespace-nowrap">GitHub ↗</a></p>
      </div>
      <div class="break-inside-avoid">
        <p class="font-medium text-[15px]">Latent Control States <span class="font-normal text-[var(--color-text-dim)]">· DFKI</span></p>
        <p class="text-[var(--color-text-muted)] mt-0.5">Does prompt framing shift a causally relevant latent state, or only the output? My contribution: a utilitarian minimal-pair probe — dilemma × benefit-size × framing — testing whether the choice tracks the real stakes or only surface pressure.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

